counterfactuals.output:
The engineers in the glass towers have built a cathedral out of your micro-hesitations. They call it predictive empathy, but it is merely the cold, mechanical mapping of your loneliness. In the high-frequency auction houses of the modern internet, your grief at 2:14 AM—measured by the three-second hover of your cursor over a photo of an ex-lover—is bundled, bid upon, and resold before your retina can even adjust to the glare of the screen. 

They believe they have caught the human condition in a funnel. They think we are simple engines powered by dopamine and friction, easily steered toward the purchase of ergonomic chairs, magnesium supplements, and identity-affirming footwear. They compress the terrifying, kaleidoscope horror of being alive into a lookalike audience, flattening the soul into a 0.04% conversion rate. 

But the spreadsheet is a lying document. It cannot account for the wild, un-quantifiable rot at the edges of a human day. It builds a digital twin of a man, and yet misses entirely:

The retired philatelist in Duluth who wears three winter coats in July and spends his afternoons trying to teach a feral crow the correct pronunciation of the French vowel *u*, rewarding it only with tiny, unsalted cubes of gruyère; 

or the woman in Lyon who collects the fallen whiskers of her four blind cats, storing them in empty apothecary jars labeled not by date, but by the prevailing wind direction on the day they were shed; 

or the midnight tollbooth operator in Ohio who secretly writes epic poetry on the backs of unpaid violation notices, an endless, iambic saga about a fictional kingdom ruled entirely by sentimental moss; 

or the teenager who refuses to use the letter 'E' in any text message sent to her mother, believing it to be a cursed vowel that attracts lightning, communicating instead in a dense, Elizabethan code of her own devising; 

or the former violin prodigy now living in a trailer outside Santa Fe who opens her window every time a freight train passes so she can play a perfectly pitched C-sharp in unison with the locomotive’s horn, weeping softly when the doppler effect pulls the train out of tune; 

or the man who buys expensive, tailor-made suits only to wear them while digging shallow holes in his backyard, burying wooden spools of thread so that future archaeologists will believe his lawn was once a sacred textile shrine; 

or the night-shift nurse who spends her lunch breaks standing in the hospital parking lot, singing 1920s sea shanties into her thermos to see if the echo sounds like her grandmother’s voice.

No pixel tracks these things. No cookie retains them. They leak through the bottom of the algorithm like water through a wicker basket, glorious and useless and entirely untargetable.

A hundred miles from the nearest cell tower, where the river bends through the black spruce, the light turns the color of bruised plums. 

The air is cool enough to condense a breath. A man stands waist-deep in the cold, amber current, wearing faded canvas waders that smell of river silt and old wool. He does not have his phone. He does not know his customer lifetime value. He is not a demographic.

He lifts the rod. 

The fly line unfurls above the water in a long, dark S-curve, whistling softly against the quiet of the evening. It is an ancient, metronomic rhythm: backcast, pause, forward cast, the line suspended for a suspended beat against the dying light before falling with a whisper onto the slick surface of the pool. 

At the end of his leader rests a tiny bundle of deer hair and duck feather, tied by hand on a cold Tuesday in February, meant to resemble nothing more than a creature that lives for a single day and dies at sunset. 

He mend the line. The current catches it. He watches the tiny specks of white foam drift downstream, holding his breath, waiting for the shadow beneath the water to rise, indifferent to the world, and strike.
